/*!
@defgroup crc
@ingroup mod_security Cyclic Redundancy Check (CRC)
@brief Hardware-accelerated CRC computation module for the YTMicro SDK.

@details
The CRC module provides a hardware-based cyclic redundancy check engine
that computes checksums over arbitrary data streams. It is commonly used for
data integrity verification in communication protocols, flash programming,
and memory protection.

@section crc_features Key Features
- Hardware-accelerated CRC computation (single-cycle per data word).
- Supports **CRC-16** and **CRC-32** protocols, with optional **CRC-4** and
  **CRC-8** on select devices.
- Configurable initial seed (starting checksum value).
- Configurable bit-order transpose (reflection) on input and output paths.
- Optional output complement (final XOR) for protocols that require it.
- Accepts 8-bit, 16-bit, or 32-bit data input widths.
- Supports streaming (multi-block) CRC computation — data can be fed
  incrementally without restarting the calculation.

@section crc_standards Supported CRC Standards

The following table lists common CRC standards and their corresponding
configuration parameter values for the crc_user_config_t structure:

| Standard                  | crcWidth     | seed       | writeTranspose       | readTranspose        | complementChecksum |
|---------------------------|--------------|------------|----------------------|----------------------|--------------------|
| CRC-16-CCITT (0x1021)     | CRC_BITS_16  | 0xFFFF     | CRC_TRANSPOSE_BITS   | CRC_TRANSPOSE_NONE   | false              |
| CRC-16-IBM (0x8005)       | CRC_BITS_16  | 0x0000     | CRC_TRANSPOSE_BITS   | CRC_TRANSPOSE_BITS   | false              |
| CRC-32 (0x04C11DB7)       | CRC_BITS_32  | 0xFFFFFFFF | CRC_TRANSPOSE_BITS   | CRC_TRANSPOSE_BITS   | true               |
| CRC-32/ITU-T V.42         | CRC_BITS_32  | 0xFFFFFFFF | CRC_TRANSPOSE_BITS   | CRC_TRANSPOSE_BITS   | true               |

@warning When generating CRC-32 for the ITU-T V.42 standard, you **must** set
SWAP_BYTEWISE (readTranspose) together with INV (complementChecksum) and
SWAP_IN (writeTranspose). Failure to do so produces incorrect results.

@warning When generating CRC-16-CCITT (0x1021), you **must** set the
SWAP_BITWISE bit (writeTranspose = CRC_TRANSPOSE_BITS).

@section crc_arch Module Architecture

The CRC module is organized in a two-layer architecture:

@verbatim
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│              Application Code                │
│         (calls CRC_DRV_* functions)          │
├──────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│          CRC Driver Layer (crc_driver.h/c)   │
│  ● Instance-based API (CRC_DRV_Init, etc.)   │
│  ● Uses instance index (0..N) to resolve     │
│    peripheral base address                   │
├──────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│     CRC HW Access Layer (crc_hw_access.h/c)  │
│  ● Direct register-level access (CRC_Init,   │
│    CRC_SetDataReg, CRC_GetCrcResult, etc.)   │
│  ● Mostly inline functions for performance   │
├──────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│           CRC Peripheral Hardware            │
│         (DATA, INIT, RESULT, CTRL regs)      │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────┘
@endverbatim

@subsection crc_arch_driver Driver Layer (crc_driver.h)
Application code should **only** use the `CRC_DRV_*` functions from crc_driver.h.
These functions accept a zero-based **instance** index and resolve the hardware
base address internally using `CRC_BASE_PTRS`.

@subsection crc_arch_hw HW Access Layer (crc_hw_access.h)
This internal layer provides low-level inline register accessors. It is
organized into five functional groups:
- **Initialization** — CRC_Init()
- **Data Input** — CRC_SetDataReg(), CRC_SetDataReg16(), CRC_SetDataReg8()
- **Result & Seed** — CRC_GetCrcResult(), CRC_GetResultReg(), CRC_GetSeedReg(), CRC_SetSeedReg()
- **Protocol Config** — CRC_SetProtocolWidth(), CRC_GetProtocolWidth(), CRC_SetFXorMode(), CRC_GetFXorMode()
- **Transpose Config** — CRC_SetWriteTranspose(), CRC_GetWriteTranspose(), CRC_SetReadTranspose(), CRC_GetReadTranspose()

@section crc_config Configuration Parameters

@subsection crc_config_struct crc_user_config_t Fields
| Field              | Type             | Description                                                                    | Default Value                        |
|--------------------|------------------|-------------------------------------------------                               |--------------------------------------|
| crcWidth           | crc_bit_width_t  | CRC polynomial width (CRC_BITS_16 / CRC_BITS_32; CRC_BITS_4 / CRC_BITS_8 on supported devices). | FEATURE_CRC_DEFAULT_WIDTH (CRC_BITS_16) |
| readTranspose      | crc_transpose_t  | Bit-order transpose applied to the CRC result on readback.                     | FEATURE_CRC_DEFAULT_READ_TRANSPOSE (NONE) |
| writeTranspose     | crc_transpose_t  | Bit-order transpose applied to input data before CRC computation.              | FEATURE_CRC_DEFAULT_WRITE_TRANSPOSE (NONE) |
| complementChecksum | bool             | If true, CRC result is complemented (XORed with 0xFFFF / 0xFFFFFFFF) on read. | false                                |
| seed               | uint32_t         | Initial checksum value. Determines starting state of the CRC engine.           | FEATURE_CRC_DEFAULT_SEED (0xFFFF)    |

@subsection crc_config_features Device Feature Macros
| Macro                              | Description                                           |
|------------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------|
| FEATURE_CRC_DEFAULT_WIDTH          | Default CRC width (typically CRC_BITS_16).            |
| FEATURE_CRC_DEFAULT_READ_TRANSPOSE | Default read transpose (typically CRC_TRANSPOSE_NONE).|
| FEATURE_CRC_DEFAULT_WRITE_TRANSPOSE| Default write transpose (typically CRC_TRANSPOSE_NONE).|
| FEATURE_CRC_DEFAULT_POLYNOMIAL     | Default polynomial value (typically 0x1021).          |
| FEATURE_CRC_DEFAULT_SEED           | Default seed value (typically 0xFFFF).                |
| FEATURE_CRC_SUPPORT_CRC4           | Set to 1 if device supports 4-bit CRC mode.          |
| FEATURE_CRC_SUPPORT_CRC8           | Set to 1 if device supports 8-bit CRC mode.          |
| FEATURE_CRC_DRIVER_SOFT_POLYNOMIAL | Defined if device uses software-configurable poly.    |

@section crc_usage Usage Patterns

@subsection crc_usage_basic Pattern 1: Basic Initialization and Block CRC Calculation
The most common usage: initialize with a standard CRC configuration, feed a
data buffer, and retrieve the checksum.

@code{.c}
#define INST_CRC (0U)

crc_user_config_t crcConfig;
uint8_t buffer[] = { 0x31, 0x32, 0x33, 0x34, 0x35, 0x36, 0x37, 0x38, 0x39 };
uint32_t result;

/* Step 1: Get default configuration (CRC-16-CCITT, seed=0xFFFF) */
CRC_DRV_GetDefaultConfig(&crcConfig);

/* Step 2: Initialize CRC instance */
CRC_DRV_Init(INST_CRC, &crcConfig);

/* Step 3: Optionally reconfigure (e.g., change to CRC-32) */
/* CRC_DRV_Configure(INST_CRC, &crcConfig); */

/* Step 4: Feed data */
CRC_DRV_WriteData(INST_CRC, buffer, sizeof(buffer));

/* Step 5: Get CRC result */
result = CRC_DRV_GetCrcResult(INST_CRC);

/* Step 6: De-initialize when done */
CRC_DRV_Deinit(INST_CRC);
@endcode

@subsection crc_usage_single Pattern 2: Single-Value Quick CRC (32/16/8-bit)
When you only need to compute the CRC of a single data word without full
init/deinit overhead:

@code{.c}
#define INST_CRC (0U)

uint32_t seed = 0xFFFFU;
uint32_t data32 = 0x12345678U;
uint16_t data16 = 0x1234U;
uint32_t result;

/* CRC of a single 32-bit word, with new seed */
result = CRC_DRV_GetCrc32(INST_CRC, data32, true, seed);

/* CRC of a single 16-bit half-word, with new seed */
result = CRC_DRV_GetCrc16(INST_CRC, data16, true, seed);

/* CRC of a single 8-bit byte, continuing previous calculation (no new seed) */
result = CRC_DRV_GetCrc8(INST_CRC, 0x42U, false, 0U);
@endcode

@subsection crc_usage_stream Pattern 3: Streaming (Multi-Block) CRC
Feed data in multiple segments. The CRC accumulates across calls as long
as the seed is not re-written between them:

@code{.c}
#define INST_CRC (0U)

crc_user_config_t crcConfig;
CRC_DRV_GetDefaultConfig(&crcConfig);
CRC_DRV_Init(INST_CRC, &crcConfig);

/* First segment */
uint8_t segment1[] = { 0x01, 0x02, 0x03, 0x04 };
CRC_DRV_WriteData(INST_CRC, segment1, 4U);

/* Optional: read intermediate CRC */
uint32_t intermediateCrc = CRC_DRV_GetCrcResult(INST_CRC);

/* Second segment — CRC continues from previous state */
uint8_t segment2[] = { 0x05, 0x06, 0x07, 0x08 };
CRC_DRV_WriteData(INST_CRC, segment2, 4U);

/* Final CRC over all 8 bytes */
uint32_t finalCrc = CRC_DRV_GetCrcResult(INST_CRC);

CRC_DRV_Deinit(INST_CRC);
@endcode

@subsection crc_usage_reconfig Pattern 4: Runtime Reconfiguration
Switch between CRC protocols at runtime without de-init:

@code{.c}
#define INST_CRC (0U)

crc_user_config_t crcConfig;
uint32_t result16, result32;
uint8_t data[] = { 0xAA, 0xBB, 0xCC, 0xDD };

/* --- CRC-16-CCITT calculation --- */
CRC_DRV_GetDefaultConfig(&crcConfig);  /* CRC-16 by default */
CRC_DRV_Init(INST_CRC, &crcConfig);
CRC_DRV_WriteData(INST_CRC, data, sizeof(data));
result16 = CRC_DRV_GetCrcResult(INST_CRC);

/* --- Switch to CRC-32 --- */
crcConfig.crcWidth = CRC_BITS_32;
crcConfig.seed = 0xFFFFFFFFU;
crcConfig.complementChecksum = true;
crcConfig.writeTranspose = CRC_TRANSPOSE_BITS;
crcConfig.readTranspose = CRC_TRANSPOSE_BITS;
CRC_DRV_Configure(INST_CRC, &crcConfig);  /* Reconfigure in-place */
CRC_DRV_WriteData(INST_CRC, data, sizeof(data));
result32 = CRC_DRV_GetCrcResult(INST_CRC);

CRC_DRV_Deinit(INST_CRC);
@endcode

@section crc_api API Quick Reference

@subsection crc_api_init Initialization & De-initialization
| Function                    | Description                                                    |
|-----------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------|
| CRC_DRV_Init()              | Initialize CRC instance with user configuration.               |
| CRC_DRV_Deinit()            | Reset CRC instance to default state.                           |
| CRC_DRV_GetDefaultConfig()  | Fill configuration struct with device defaults.                |

@subsection crc_api_config Configuration
| Function                    | Description                                                    |
|-----------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------|
| CRC_DRV_Configure()         | Apply configuration to CRC hardware at runtime.                |
| CRC_DRV_GetConfig()         | Read current configuration from CRC hardware.                  |

@subsection crc_api_calc CRC Calculation
| Function                    | Description                                                    |
|-----------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------|
| CRC_DRV_GetCrc32()          | Compute CRC of one 32-bit word (optionally with new seed).     |
| CRC_DRV_GetCrc16()          | Compute CRC of one 16-bit half-word (optionally with new seed).|
| CRC_DRV_GetCrc8()           | Compute CRC of one 8-bit byte (optionally with new seed).      |
| CRC_DRV_WriteData()         | Feed a byte array into the running CRC calculation.            |
| CRC_DRV_WriteData16()       | Feed a half-word array into the running CRC calculation.       |
| CRC_DRV_WriteData32()       | Feed a word array into the running CRC calculation.            |
| CRC_DRV_GetCrcResult()      | Retrieve the current CRC result.                               |

@section crc_notes Important Notes

@subsection crc_notes_clock Clock Dependency
The CRC peripheral clock must be enabled via the clock_manager **before**
calling CRC_DRV_Init(). The driver does not manage clocks internally.
See @ref clock_manager.

@subsection crc_notes_transpose Transpose and XOR Requirements
- **CRC-32 ITU-T V.42**: requires `writeTranspose = CRC_TRANSPOSE_BITS`,
  `readTranspose = CRC_TRANSPOSE_BITS`, and `complementChecksum = true`.
  Omitting any of these produces incorrect checksums.
- **CRC-16-CCITT (0x1021)**: requires `writeTranspose = CRC_TRANSPOSE_BITS`.

@subsection crc_notes_device Device Variations
Not all CRC bit widths are available on all devices:
- **CRC-4**: only available when `FEATURE_CRC_SUPPORT_CRC4 == 1`.
- **CRC-8**: only available when `FEATURE_CRC_SUPPORT_CRC8 == 1`.
- Check the device-specific `<device>_features.h` header for support.

@subsection crc_notes_streaming Streaming CRC Behavior
When using CRC_DRV_WriteData() in streaming mode (Pattern 3), do **not** call
CRC_DRV_Configure() between data segments, because it writes a new seed and
resets the running checksum. Use CRC_DRV_GetCrcResult() to inspect intermediate
values without disrupting the calculation.

@section crc_integration Integration Guide

@subsection crc_integration_files Compilation Units
The following source files must be added to the project build:
@verbatim
${SDK_PATH}/platform/drivers/src/crc/crc_driver.c
${SDK_PATH}/platform/drivers/src/crc/crc_hw_access.c
@endverbatim

@subsection crc_integration_include Include Paths
Add the following directory to the toolchain include path:
@verbatim
${SDK_PATH}/platform/drivers/inc/
@endverbatim

@subsection crc_integration_symbols Compile Symbols
No special compile-time symbols are required for this module. Device-specific
feature macros (`FEATURE_CRC_*`) are automatically defined in the device
features header.

@subsection crc_integration_deps Dependencies
- @ref clock_manager — Required for enabling the CRC peripheral clock.

*/
